# Git & Release Workflow

## Before first code edit (fixes & features) — REQUIRED

Use this for any **bugfix**, **feature**, or **multi-file / non-trivial** implementation. Skip only for **read-only** tasks or when the user explicitly says "no branch" / "edit on `main` only".

**Never edit implementation work directly on `main`.** If the current branch is already not `main`, continue on that branch by default even when the troubleshooting target changes before GA; do **not** create a second topic branch unless the user explicitly asks or the work must be isolated. Use separate commits with clear messages to distinguish different bugfix/feature phases on the same pre-GA branch.

1. **Do not** use repo-editing tools (`apply_patch`, scripted bulk edits, etc.) while on `main` (see also: *Never commit fixes or features directly on `main`* under canary flow below).
2. **If currently on `main`, branch from the current HEAD before editing** — never switch away and back just to branch:
   - `git checkout -b fix/<short-slug>` **or** `git checkout -b feat/<short-slug>` directly from the current `main` HEAD.
   - Do **not** `git checkout main && git pull` unless the user explicitly asks to sync first.
3. **If currently on a non-`main` branch, continue there by default.** This still applies when the immediate target changes from one bug or feature to another before GA. Do not create a new branch just because the next fix touches a different area; separate the phases with distinct commits. All non-`main` branches are pre-GA work branches; they should be merged to `main` for GA and deleted afterward.
4. **Uncommitted changes on `main`:** run **`git checkout -b fix/<slug>`** (or `feat/<slug>`) *before* continuing edits. If Git blocks branch creation with a dirty tree, **`git stash push`**, create the branch, then **`git stash pop`**.
5. **Verify before editing:** `git branch --show-current` — must **not** be `main` (unless the user explicitly asked to work on `main`).

## Branch cleanup — one active branch at a time

At most **one** `feat/*` or `fix/*` branch should exist for a given line of work. Avoid accumulating stale branches.

### When creating a new branch that supersedes a previous one

If the new branch is created from HEAD and the old branch's commits are already included in that HEAD:

1. Delete the **old** branch locally and on `origin`:
   ```bash
   git branch -D <old-branch>
   git push origin --delete <old-branch>
   ```
2. Verify: `git branch -a | grep <old-branch>` should return nothing.

### After merging to `main`

Once a feature or fix branch has been merged into `main`, delete that branch locally and on `origin` (no need to wait for GA — cleanup is immediate). The "After GA" section below has the detailed commands.

## Planning (Plan mode / implementation plans)

When producing or updating an **implementation plan** (e.g. Cursor Plan mode / `CreatePlan`), include a short **Git workflow** subsection aligned with *Before first code edit* above: if currently on `main`, `git checkout -b` a `feat/<slug>` or `fix/<slug>` before edits; if already on a non-`main` branch, continue there by default even when the target changes, using distinct commits for each phase. Never plan work as if it will land directly on `main`. Note that completed fixes/features are committed locally in every affected Git repository after verification, including the separate `wiki/` repository when it changed, while **push / tag / release** actions require an explicit in-turn user instruction (*Agent commit policy* below).

## Agent commit policy (STRICT)

**After one bugfix or feature is complete, verified, and documented as required,
stage only that work and create local commits automatically in every affected
Git repository.** Do not wait for another user prompt. If both the main
repository and the separate `wiki/` repository changed, each must receive its
own local commit containing only its relevant files. A later bugfix or feature
must receive new local commits; do not amend or fold it into previous completed
work.

Treat each completed implementation or review-fix pass as a separate **round**.
This applies whether work started from an implementation plan, direct bug
report, code review, CI review, or follow-up review of an earlier local commit.
After a round is verified and documented, create a new local commit on top of
the previous round's commit. Never leave a completed review-fix round
uncommitted, amend it into the prior commit, or wait to combine it with a later
round.

- While a fix/feature is still in progress, keep edits uncommitted so verification
  and related corrections stay in one coherent commit.
- Before the automatic local commit, inspect status and diff, preserve unrelated
  work, stage only files belonging to the completed fix/feature, and use the
  required gitmoji commit-message style.
- Documentation required by the completed change belongs in the same logical
  local commit when it lives in the main repository. When the GitHub Wiki is
  updated in its separate repository, its relevant Wiki pages **must also be
  committed locally before completion**; preserve unrelated Wiki edits.
- Rule/docs-only maintenance requested as a standalone task is also committed
  locally after verification.
- **Never run `git push`, create or push a tag, trigger a remote workflow, create
  a GitHub release, or otherwise modify a remote without an explicit in-turn
  user instruction.** Earlier approval to commit locally does not authorize any
  remote action.
- Never auto-bump the canary number between fixes. Wait for the user to
  confirm that the fix is ready to ship.

## Commit preflight — one batched read, never repeat

Run the pre-commit inspection **once**, as a single batch: `git status`, the
staged/unstaged `git diff`, and `git log` for message style. These are
independent read-only commands, so issue them in one message (parallel calls or
one chained command) for every affected repository, including `wiki/`.

Hard gates:

- Once a read-only check has produced output, the only legal next moves are
  **forward** (stage, commit, verify) or **stop and report**. Re-running it is
  not a move.
- If a read-only command returns output identical to its previous run and no
  files were edited in between, treat that as a control-flow bug: act on the
  evidence already gathered, or tell the user what is blocking progress.
- Never poll `git status` (or any idempotent inspection) while deciding what to
  commit. It wastes tokens, floods the transcript, and hides real progress.
- Post-commit verification is a **different** state from preflight: one
  `git status` plus `git log -1` per repository after committing is expected,
  and does not count as a repeat.

## Commits

- Commit messages must be prefixed with a **gitmoji** (e.g. `🐛 fix(scope): …`, `✨ feat(scope): …`).
- Use a new commit for every later fix or feature, even when continuing on the
  same pre-GA branch.
- Review follow-ups are later fix rounds: commit each verified round on top of
  the commit it reviews; never amend or squash it into that earlier commit.
- Before merge: show `git status` and `git branch -a`.
- After merge/push: run `git fetch --prune` to confirm remote branch cleanup.
- After merge: verify with `git log --oneline -3`.
- Before `git stash push/pop`: run `git stash list`.

## Release triggers

Docker builds run **only on tag pushes**, not on commits to `main`.
`.github/workflows/docker-release.yml` reacts to:

- `v*.*.*` → pushes `lqdflying/chathub:X.Y.Z` **and** `:latest` (GA only)
- `v*.*.*-canary.*` → pushes `lqdflying/chathub:X.Y.Z-canary.N` (does **not** touch `:latest`)

Version scheme: `v1.x.x` (ChatHub independent versioning, unrelated to upstream v1.x).

**Docker build reliability:** `.github/workflows/docker-release.yml` frees unused runner disk (preinstalled SDK trees, `docker system prune`) before `docker build` so large Next.js images are less likely to hit **"No space left on device"** on `ubuntu-latest`.

## Mandatory build-trigger preflight

If the user asks to **"trigger build"**, **"trigger image build"**, **"new image"**, **"test build"**, **"canary"**, **"ship"**, or **"commit and push to trigger build"**, treat that as a request for the canary flow unless the change is docs/rules-only.

Before running `git push` or `git tag` for that request (and before any local
commit still needed for uncommitted work), the agent must re-read this file's
**Release triggers**, **Docs / rules-only commits**, and **Fix / feature canary
flow** sections and state the intended action:

- current branch and whether it is non-`main`
- whether the staged diff is docs/rules-only or contains code/config
- commit message, including the required gitmoji prefix
- whether a Docker image build is expected
- exact canary tag to push, or `no tag` with the docs/rules-only reason

Hard gates:

- A **branch push is never a Docker image trigger**.
- If code/config changed and the user asked to trigger a build, push a valid `vX.Y.Z-canary.N` tag after the branch push.
- If the change is docs/rules-only, do not push a canary tag and do not claim a Docker image build was triggered.
- After pushing a canary tag, verify the fork workflow with:
  ```bash
  gh run list -R lqdflying/chathub --workflow=docker-release.yml --limit 3
  ```
- The final response for a canary flow must include the pushed tag and the workflow run id/status.

## Docs / rules-only commits — no tag needed

Commits that change **only** documentation or rules files (`.md`, `.mdc` in `.cursor/rules/`, wiki, etc.) do NOT need a canary tag. These commits don't affect the Docker image output, so triggering a Docker build is wasteful.

- Create the local commit automatically after verification.
- Push that commit only when the user explicitly requests a remote update.
- Do not create or push a tag.
- Example: `📝 docs: add PWA icon style rule`
- Example: `📝 docs: update git workflow rules`

If other functional changes are mixed in (code, icons, config), the canary flow still applies.

## Fix / feature canary flow (default)

Use this sequence **only when the user explicitly asks to ship / tag / cut a
canary**. Never kick it off on your own — the STRICT policy above wins for
both canary and formal tags.

**Never commit fixes or features directly on `main` just to get an image.**

1. Ensure the work is on a non-`main` branch (`fix/<slug>` or `feat/<slug>`). If already on a non-`main` branch, use it.
2. Verify the completed fix/feature already has its automatic local commit. If
   shipping uncovered additional required edits, verify and create a new local
   commit for those edits with a gitmoji prefix. **Do not** bump `package.json`.
3. Pick the canary version — always a pre-release of the **next unreleased** version, never the current GA:
   - Read `package.json` version (= last GA, e.g. `3.4.38`).
   - If `git tag -l vX.Y.Z` matches → that GA shipped → canary = `vX.Y.(Z+1)-canary.N`.
   - Otherwise canary = `vX.Y.Z-canary.N`.
   - `N` = (highest existing `vX.Y.Z-canary.*` for that version) + 1:
     `git tag -l 'vX.Y.Z-canary.*' | sort -V | tail`
4. Push branch, then tag:
   ```bash
   git push -u origin <branch>
   git tag vX.Y.Z-canary.N
   git push origin vX.Y.Z-canary.N
   ```
5. Verify the build on the **fork**, not upstream:
   ```bash
   gh run list -R lqdflying/chathub --workflow=docker-release.yml --limit 3
   ```
6. Do not merge the branch to `main` until the canary image has been tested.

## Recover from a wrong tag

```bash
gh run cancel <run-id> -R lqdflying/chathub
git push --delete origin vBAD && git tag -d vBAD
git tag vGOOD && git push origin vGOOD
```

## GA promotion (only after canary passes)

Trigger only on explicit user instruction (e.g. "promote to GA", "bump to
vX.Y.Z and tag"). Do not auto-promote even when the canary looks green.

**Typical flow:** merge the feature branch into **`main`** (fast-forward or merge commit), then on **`main`** bump **`package.json`**, commit, tag **`vX.Y.Z`**, push **`main`** and the tag. Ensure `main` contains all GA work before tagging.

```bash
# On main after merge — replace old/new version strings
sed -i 's/"version": "old"/"version": "new"/' package.json
git add package.json && git commit -m "🔖 chore: bump version to vX.Y.Z"
git tag vX.Y.Z && git push origin main && git push origin vX.Y.Z
```

(This replaces older one-liners that pushed `HEAD:main` from a non-main branch — prefer **checkout `main`**, merge, bump, tag from **`main`**.)

This is the **only** flow allowed to push a non-canary `v*.*.*` tag and update `:latest`.

### GitHub Release (MANDATORY — part of GA)

Every GA tag **must** be accompanied by a `gh release create`. The release description must summarize all changes since the previous GA release.

1. Find the **previous GA tag**:
   ```bash
   gh release list -R lqdflying/chathub --limit 5
   # or: git tag -l 'v*' --sort=-v:refname | grep -v 'canary' | head -2
   ```
2. Generate the **release notes** by reviewing all commits between the previous GA and the new tag:
   ```bash
   git log --oneline vPREV..vNEW
   ```
3. Create the release:
   ```bash
   gh release create vX.Y.Z -R lqdflying/chathub \
     --title "vX.Y.Z — <short summary>" \
     --notes "$(cat <<'EOF'
   ## Highlights

   - summarized from git log between previous GA and this tag
   - group by category (Bug Fix, Feature, etc.)

   ## What's Changed

   (details...)

   ---

   Full diff: https://github.com/lqdflying/chathub/compare/vPREV...vNEW
   EOF
   )"
   ```

Do **not** create the release before pushing the tag — the tag push triggers the Docker build. Create the release after the tag push.

### After GA: clean up merged branches (MANDATORY)

After a GA tag has shipped, **immediately delete the merged feature/fix branch** — do not leave it to accumulate. This is a mandatory part of the GA promotion workflow, not an optional cleanup. **Do not wait for the user to ask.** Proactively clean up after completing the GA steps (merge, bump, tag, push, GitHub Release).

Also clean up any stale local-only branches (e.g. from previous iterations of the same line of work) that are ancestors of `main`.

**Do not delete** `main` or any branches the user is actively working on.

#### Identify merged branches

```bash
git fetch --prune origin
git branch --merged main    # local branches fully merged into main (except main itself)
git branch -a | grep -E 'fix/|feat/'  # spot stale remotes
```

#### Delete locally

```bash
git branch -d <branch>      # preferred when Git agrees it is merged
git branch -D <branch>      # only if work is already on main but Git refuses -d
```

#### Delete on `origin`

```bash
git push origin --delete <branch> [<branch> ...]
```

#### Verify

```bash
git fetch --prune origin
git branch -a               # should show only `main` and `remotes/origin/main`
```
